    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nVolvo EX60 Manufacturing Difficulties Hinder Production Output, US Dealers Hit Worst\n14 photos\nPhoto: Volvo\nVolvo's most important electric vehicle yet is running at roughly a tenth of its planned output. As per a source cited by Automotive News, the EX60 line in Torslanda is building just around 10 percent of the 2,000 crossovers a week Volvo initially targeted, leaving the US dealership network without significant volume until deep into 2027.\nAbout 500 units were delivered worldwide in July 2026, while August 2026 saw an estimated 250 deliveries. Volvo told its US-based dealers to plan for approximately 7,000 sales in 2027 alone, a figure that has recently morphed from a target to wishful thinking. Series production of the EX60 kicked off in the latter part of April 2026, meaning that the EX60 line has had about half a year to find its rhythm.\nThe weekly target of 2,000 units would have meant in the ballpark of 8,000 units per month, but alas, the summer tally ran to hundreds instead of thousands. Rather than the vehicle per se, manufacturing difficulties hindered the EX60's rollout. According to the insider cited by Automotive News, the problems concern the cell-to-body integration and the megacasting process.\nBoth have proven more complex to scale than originally assumed, and Volvo's paper trail is pretty blunt. In a memo sent to US dealers, the Swedish manufacturer acknowledges that introducing a brand-new platform and model into a running factory is always a challenge. That memo was sent to US retailers in September 2026.\nIn it, Volvo highlights that the delays are not related to product performance, safety, or quality. All in all, it's easy to understand why the ramp-up phase hasn't unfolded as expected. The company is presently prioritizing European markets, where electric vehicle adoption is much stronger than in the United States and Canada.\nWhat that means is limited availability in North America into 2027, but a significant number of already allocated vehicles are certain to reach American retailers early next year. Shipments are expected to go back to normal in the second half of 2027. For dealers, Volvo is leaving a bunch of potential sales on the table while other legacy brands arrive with brand-new EVs of their own.\nPhoto: Volvo This wouldn't be the first time a Volvo electric model's rollout was hindered by problems. Remember the EX90? It launched much later than anticipated due to quality concerns, missing features, and software glitches, an experience that left many retailers wary of a repeat. Who could blame them? After all, the production-related issues of the more affordable EX60 proved them right.\nCompeting in the largest premium segment, the EX60 anchors the Swedish automaker's target of 200,000 annual US retail sales by 2030. This number looks increasingly distant with each compromised launch, especially after Volvo posted an 11 percent nosedive in global deliveries in the past quarter. China dropped the hardest, with Volvo noting a drop of 41 percent. US volume, on the other hand, slid a more manageable 8.7 percent.\nVolvo has understandably scrapped its 2026 cash flow outlook, with Volvo also freezing white-collar hiring globally while also tightening outside spending. Meanwhile, the XC60 and the XC90 both delivered year-over-year growth in the US market in the third quarter of 2026.\nTheir plug-in hybrid versions deliver up to 78 and 73 miles of electric range, respectively, meaning 126 and 117 kilometers in metric speak. Both Mercedes and BMW are already shipping similarly sized premium electric crossovers, making the EX60's delay all the more unkind to dealers nationwide.\nBefore this stumble, chief exec Hakan Samuelsson projected about 60,000 global sales for the EX60 this year alone. With the EX60 assembly line running at a tenth of the projected weekly output, first-year production numbers will be truly disappointing. On the upside, kudos to Volvo for sorting things out instead of passing potential problems onto consumers.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolvo Volvo EX60 EV US production\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Car Guy Got 1969 Charger R/T Hemi 4-Speed After Watching Bullitt in 1975, He Loves Driving It Still",
